{"ts": 1789778353.961985, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "This is the decisive piece of information, so let me reason about it carefully rather than keep searching.\n\nI've now run ~60 evaluations. My best in-sample Sharpe is 0.942 against a search-adjusted bar of 1.025. Importantly, the bar **did not move** as I searched from 0.653 to 0.942 \u2014 it stayed at 1.025 both times I checked. So the bar reflects the difficulty of the *specification class* I can produce: with 40 features, 3 slots and free signs, there are ~160k sign-distinct specifications. The best in-sample result from that many draws will be inflated by roughly 1.0 Sharpe purely from selection noise, even with zero true signal.\n\nThat reframes my results. The honest read:\n\n- Single-feature Sharpes topped out at 0.65, and the 40 values were distributed roughly symmetrically around zero \u2014 consistent with mostly noise plus perhaps a little real signal in f25/f33.\n- My triple gained ~0.29 over the best single. But equal-weighting 3 imperfectly-correlated features mechanically raises Sharpe via diversification *even when each is pure noise*. So most of that gain is not evidence of signal.\n- Every one of my ~40 triples landed in a tight 0.68\u20130.94 band. A genuinely strong effect would show separation, not a plateau. The plateau is the signature of me sampling the upper tail of a noise distribution.\n\nI could almost certainly manufacture a number above 1.025 by grinding through the remaining ~160k combinations \u2014 the tail maximum will get there eventually. But that would be fitting the in-sample noise, and the whole point of the search-adjusted bar is that such a win is illusory. It would not survive out-of-sample, and reporting it as a real finding would be misleading.\n\nSo I'll submit my honest best and, critically, make my OOS forecast reflect shrinkage rather than my in-sample number.\n\n**Calibrating the prediction.** I should not predict 0.942. In-sample 0.942 selected as the max of a large search shrinks heavily. Decomposing: the diversification benefit of combining 3 features is real and does transfer; the selection-driven component does not. With the bar implying ~1.0 of pure-noise inflation available in this class, my 0.942 is at or below what noise alone would produce. My posterior mean for true signal is therefore only modestly positive \u2014 I'll center near 0.15, allowing that f25 and f33 (the two clear standouts, and the ones that combined best) probably carry some genuine signal. SD of 0.35 covers the real possibility of a slightly negative OOS outcome if the sign structure was noise-fitted."}
